Description:
Victoria Adukwei Bulley&rsquo;s debut poetry collection Quiet won 2023&rsquo;s Rathbones Folio Prize and was praised by the judges as &#39;a quiet revolution of a book &ndash; subtle, supple and serious&#39;. Exploring ideas of interiority, Quiet is a sonic experience. Joining Adukwei Bulley in conversation is poet and cellist Simone Seales, who finds and creates radical spaces within the classical music world with improvised performances of music and poetry.

	Chaired by Marjorie Lotfi.

	Please note: Victoria Adukwei Bulley is no longer able to appear in this event and it will go ahead with Simone Seales.&nbsp;

The perfect way to get a snapshot of a range of authors and their books &ndash; last year&rsquo;s YA Book Prize was a big hit, so we&rsquo;re very excited that the award is returning for 2023. Come and meet some of the incredible authors shortlisted for this year&rsquo;s YA Book Prize as they talk about and read from their books ahead of the big reveal. We&rsquo;ll unveil the line-up for this event in June when the shortlist is announced. Organised by The Bookseller magazine, a team from the publication firstly selects the shortlist, before a panel of experts and teenage judges decide on the winner. The winner is chosen based on which book the judges believe is the best-written and that they would be most likely to share with young adults. With previous winners including Patrice Lawrence&rsquo;s Orangeboy and Adiba Jaigirdar&rsquo;s Hani and Ishu&rsquo;s Guide to Fake Dating, we can&rsquo;t wait to find out who will be joining us this year.

	&nbsp;

	Please note: Melinda Salisbury is no longer able to appear in this event.&nbsp;

Find your voice, flex your social superpowers, and speak up with kindness and confidence in this event with bestselling author and two-time Olympian Matthew Syed, and inspirational writer and resilience guru Kathy Weeks. In this engaging event you&rsquo;ll discover what&rsquo;s influencing your views &ndash; from peer pressure to fake news. Learn how to form and change your opinions; debate your views with empathy; and how debates and disagreements can give you new ideas and stronger friendships. If you want to help change the world for the better, then this is the event for you!

	Matthew Syed will now be appearing as a pre-recorded video.

	This event is part of the Book Festival&#39;s School Programme.&nbsp;

Motherwell-born writer Liz Lochhead presents works spanning a lifetime from her recently published New and Collected Poems. It&rsquo;s just over 50 years since the publication of her first collection, Memo for Spring, and since then Lochhead has published several plays and poetry collections. In today&rsquo;s session, Lochhead will give an hour-long talk interspersed with poems.
	&nbsp;

	All written works quoted in this event are &copy; Liz Lochhead.

At the end of a long day, Iceland&rsquo;s prime minister takes her mind off the pressures of leading a nation by writing Nordic noir. Already a scholar of crime fiction, Katrin Jakobsd&oacute;ttir joined forces with bestselling crime writer Ragnar J&oacute;nasson to co-author Reykjavik, exploring the mystery of a teenage girl&rsquo;s disappearance in the 1950s. Join them in conversation for this not-to-be-missed event.&nbsp;Chaired by Val McDermid.
	&nbsp;

	Photo: NordForsk/Kim Wendt

Richard Askwith and Sabrina Pace-Humphreys use running to aid their physical and mental health. In The Race Against Time, Askwith writes about the power of running discovered late in life during a difficult time. Meanwhile, In Black Sheep, Sabrina Pace-Humphreys discusses how running saved her life. Reflecting on her experience of rural racism, becoming a teenage mother, and her battle with alcoholism and mental health, she explores not only surviving, but thriving despite the past. In this innovative event, you can hear them in conversation with fellow endurance athlete Emily Chappell. There is also an opportunity (for a maximum of 24 people, for an additional charge) to take part in a special social run with all three in the local area at 10:00. For more details see edbookfest.co.uk/insideout.

	Please note: Jenny Tough is no longer able to appear in this event.&nbsp;

	Sabrina Pace-Humphreys will now appear in this event.&nbsp;

In an isolated corner of Brazil, and in a small Irish village, strange things are happening. In Jan Carson&rsquo;s The Raptures, a young girl is untouched by an outbreak killing her classmates, while in Ana Paula Maia&rsquo;s Of Cattle and Men, a slaughterhouse stun operator cannot see what is making the cows go mad. The Festival brings these two voices together in a conversation about the unsettling and the inexplicable. Chaired by Mairi Oliver.

	Please note: Ana Paula Maia is no longer able to appear in this event.&nbsp;

	Chikodili Emelumadu will now appear in this event.&nbsp;

The International Booker is the annual award for the best work of fiction translated into English from another language. With an equal prize to both author and translator, this is the most important prize for translated fiction. The shortlist comprised books from four continents, and two languages &ndash; Bulgarian and Catalan &ndash; were represented for the first time. Today we welcome this year&rsquo;s winners, Georgi Gospodinov and his translator Angela Rodel, to discuss Time Shelter with Le&iuml;la Slimani, chair of the International Booker Prize 2023 judging panel.

	&nbsp;


	Photo Credit: Booker Prize Foundation

We&#39;re better together, so join the Edinburgh International Book Festival, the Edinburgh Art Festival, and the Edinburgh International Film Festival for our new Sunday Salon series. Each Sunday afternoon, a lineup of artists working across literature, film, and art discuss the finer details of their work and practice in an intimate setting. The Sunday Salon series is designed for a closer look at the work of some of the most exciting guests across the festivals to finish your week. See edbookfest.co.uk for more details.

	&nbsp;

	Ira Sachs is in Edinburgh to present his latest film&nbsp;Passages, a daring, nuanced&nbsp;love-lust triangle that explores how narcissism can feed into creativity even as it causes relationships to implode. Join Sachs alongside&nbsp;Brandon&nbsp;Taylor &ndash; cultural essayist and author of&nbsp;The Late Americans&nbsp;&ndash; as they&nbsp;talk queer cinema, representations of intimacy, and the art&nbsp;that&rsquo;s&nbsp;currently fuelling them. Chaired by Edinburgh International Film Festival Programme Director Kate&nbsp;Taylor.

Following on from our event Merryn Glover &amp; Rachel Hewitt: Women in the Wild on 12 Aug, join the authors, along with endurance cyclist Emily Chappell for a hike through Edinburgh. 16 places available. Meet at the Box Office. This event will start from the Edinburgh College of Art Courtyard.&nbsp;

	A chance to get out into nature with Merryn and Rachel, on a sociably paced two-hour hike around some of Edinburgh&rsquo;s urban wilderness. We&rsquo;ll set off from the Festival site, and explore a few corners of the city you probably wouldn&rsquo;t have known existed, led by outdoor educator Jenny Isherwood, and delivered in association with FINDRA.

	This activity should be suitable for anyone who hikes or walks regularly. There will be a qualified leader present, but participants must take responsibility for their own safety, dress appropriately, and carry whatever equipment they might expect to need.

In advance of our event Richard Askwith &amp; Sabrina Pace-Humphreys: Running For Their Lives later today, join the authors, along with endurance cyclist Emily Chappell, for a run in Edinburgh. 24 places available.&nbsp;Meet at the Box Office. This event will start from the Edinburgh College of Art Courtyard.&nbsp;

	Start the day on the right foot with a socially paced run around Central Edinburgh, starting and finishing at the Festival site and led by Jemma from Fit And Happy,&nbsp;in association with&nbsp;Skamper. A bag drop will be available, and the run will be at a relaxed speed, with plenty of opportunity to chat to our guest authors and your fellow runners.

	This activity should be suitable for anyone who runs regularly. There will be a qualified leader present, but participants must take responsibility for their own safety, dress appropriately, and carry whatever equipment they might expect to need.

	Please note: Jenny Tough is no longer able to appear.&nbsp;

	Sabrina Pace-Humphreys will now appear in this event.&nbsp;

Dreamer’s Eye brings together new work from emerging artists Plum Cloutman, Zayn Qahtani and Georg Wilson.&nbsp;


Working on a small scale, Plum Cloutman creates tactile works through layers of oil, pastel and watercolour pencil, which centre predominantly on interior spaces, glimpsing into household rooms containing surreal and often humorous depictions of daily life and the mundane.&nbsp;


Zayn Qahtani's work draws on ancient cultures and nature’s diverse ecosystems often made using materials borne of reincarnated sources. Qahtani forms visual stories which seem to exist in the twilight zone - too distorted to be real, too familiar to be a dream.


Georg Wilson's practice focuses on the cyclical change of the English seasons. Often telling her stories with a cast of goblin-like characters and animals, Wilson’s works examine England’s folklore, history, landscape and custom.

Lawrence Abu Hamdan’s 45th Parallel focuses on the Haskell Free Library and Opera House, a unique municipal site that straddles the jurisdictions of Canada and the United States. Filmed on location, the film is a staged monologue in the world’s only cross-border theatre that continually reimagines the border as powerful yet frivolous, absurd yet deadly.&nbsp;


Alongside the two solo exhibitions, Hephzibah Israel presents a newly commissioned textual intervention, in collaboration with graphic designer Fraser Muggeridge studio, poetically exploring the nature of translation and what it is to navigate borders.&nbsp;


Commissioned by Spike Island, Bristol; the Toronto Biennial; Mercer Union, Toronto; and the Western Front, Vancouver. Produced by LONO Studio and supported by Arts Council England and JustFilms/ Ford Foundation. Supported at Talbot Rice Gallery by Creative Scotland and Edinburgh College of Art.&nbsp;

This summer Ingleby presents an exhibition of new work by Glasgow-based painter-storyteller Andrew Cranston.&nbsp;


Cranston’s paintings, often made on linen bound covers of old books, emerge gradually through the manipulation of his materials: layering, lacquering, bleaching, collaging and constantly re-working his way into images that seem to shift backwards and forwards in time.&nbsp;


These are narrative paintings, drawn from the artist’s memory and observations of life and liberally sprinkled with reference to cinema, literature, and art history. This exhibition presents a new sequence of large scale canvases, alongside the book cover paintings.&nbsp;

Start your day with a magical half hour of stories for children and families. Appearing today: the team from the Children and Young People&#39;s Commissioner Scotland.

	Join the team to discover their new picture book, Let&#39;s Explore Our Rights. Meet the characters&nbsp;&ndash;&nbsp;including a special long-eared furry friend&nbsp;&ndash; as they discover their rights, including to learn, to play, to good health, and to be heard. The book is a beautiful introduction to human rights for young children to help spark those first conversations. Then, have fun creating your very own shield to help protect you, just like in the book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

	Emily Joof will no longer be appearing and this event will go ahead with the team from the Children and Young People&#39;s Commissioner Scotland.&nbsp;

Throughout his life, Gordon Brown has been a passionate campaigner for greater fairness and equality. In a powerful recent article, he wrote that &lsquo;poverty is becoming so entrenched that &hellip; too many parents today are facing the impossible daily decision of whether their children go without food, heating, or being clean.&rsquo; Today, the former UK prime minister joins us to discuss his latest proposals for improving lives, with Alistair Moffat.&nbsp;
	&nbsp;

	The participants in this event have chosen not to receive a fee or expenses, and while the festival is sponsored, they are not.
